WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.120
One mouse scratches normally to histamine, yet scratches much less to chloroquine.

2
00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:10.020
Did one experiment separate two neurons, or only two inputs?

3
00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.980
Watch one skin-to-cord pathway.

4
00:00:14.260 --> 00:00:19.100
Gold histamine and blue chloroquine approach the same teal sensory population.

5
00:00:20.250 --> 00:00:24.930
Researchers deleted one cluster of Mrgpr receptor genes.

6
00:00:26.190 --> 00:00:28.990
The blue chloroquine route broke, so scratching fell.

7
00:00:30.310 --> 00:00:32.050
Gold histamine scratching remained.

8
00:00:33.310 --> 00:00:35.910
The intervention separated receptor routes.

9
00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.550
It did not yet separate the neurons carrying them.

10
00:00:40.533 --> 00:00:43.853
The routes also recruit different channel machinery in mice.

11
00:00:45.313 --> 00:00:49.573
Histamine itch involves PLC-beta-3 and TRPV1.

12
00:00:50.913 --> 00:00:57.033
Chloroquine and BAM8-22 scratching instead falls markedly without TRPA1.

13
00:00:58.083 --> 00:01:02.483
Now remove every sensory neuron marked by MrgprA3.

14
00:01:03.903 --> 00:01:05.563
What should happen to histamine scratching?

15
00:01:07.343 --> 00:01:08.843
Predict before the cells disappear.

16
00:01:10.203 --> 00:01:12.603
Does the gold route survive, as it did before?

17
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:20.647
Histamine scratching also fell.

18
00:01:22.027 --> 00:01:26.947
Removing a receptor route and removing its whole neuron population are different

19
00:01:26.947 --> 00:01:31.727
interventions. The neurons receive several itch-producing inputs.

20
00:01:33.047 --> 00:01:36.567
Ablation shows they are needed for much scratching in these mouse assays.

21
00:01:37.717 --> 00:01:41.897
A second test started with mice lacking working TRPV1.

22
00:01:43.177 --> 00:01:46.157
Researchers restored that channel only in the teal neurons.

23
00:01:47.657 --> 00:01:50.397
Capsaicin could now recruit only that population.

24
00:01:51.757 --> 00:01:56.517
The mouse produced itch-related behaviour instead of its usual pain-related wiping.

25
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:02.960
The rescue shows what these neurons can drive when they alone respond to capsaicin.

26
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:06.780
It does not prove every activation means itch.

27
00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:14.030
Later, fast ionotropic stimulation mostly evoked pain-related defence.

28
00:02:14.030 --> 00:02:17.040
Gq-linked stimulation evoked itch behaviour.

29
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:19.683
Behaviour matters too.

30
00:02:20.983 --> 00:02:25.023
In the cheek assay, hindpaw scratching counts as itch-related output.

31
00:02:26.483 --> 00:02:28.903
Forelimb wiping counts as pain-related output.

32
00:02:30.143 --> 00:02:34.263
Those readouts distinguish actions, not a mouse's reported sensation.

33
00:02:36.043 --> 00:02:39.143
Human MRGPRX1 is also a different gene.

34
00:02:40.267 --> 00:02:45.127
Both receptors respond to chloroquine in transfected cells, but not equally.

35
00:02:46.487 --> 00:02:50.447
The human receptor's functional potency was lower in that assay.

36
00:02:52.087 --> 00:02:57.227
Shared ligand responses do not make the genes, neurons, or sensations equivalent

37
00:02:57.227 --> 00:02:58.227
across species.

38
00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:01.220
Here is the rule for this lesson.

39
00:03:02.780 --> 00:03:06.980
Trigger, recruited population, spinal route, and inhibition together shape

40
00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:11.480
scratching. Each perturbation supports only the link it tests.

41
00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:15.920
Chapter 2 will carry this pathway into the spinal cord.
